## This machine: where a terminal shows up in Herdr.app

hc has no notion of windows, spaces or tabs, and `herdr-app` (which arranges them)
exists only on the machine running Herdr.app, not on an SSH host. Herdr.app places
each hc terminal by its labels instead:

| How it was started | Where it appears |
| --- | --- |
| `--label herdr.space=<id the window hasn't seen>` | **its own space** (a sidebar entry), named after its `--cwd` folder |
| no space label, started from a terminal (the default) | **a tab in the parent's space**, i.e. in the user's current workspace |
| no space label, `--no-parent` | hidden from the window (still in `hc ls`) |

Unlabelled `hc run` calls are why tabs pile up in the user's space. So:

- **A worker, reviewer, long-lived agent or anything the user should be able to find:**
  start it in its own space with `hc-space` (in `~/.local/bin`), which is
  `hc run --bg --outlive-parent --label herdr.space=<fresh id>` plus your arguments.
  It prints `space: sp…` on stderr; pass `HC_SPACE=sp…` to put a second terminal (its
  dev server) in the same space.

  ```sh
  hc-space --name fix-x --cwd ~/.forest/repo/fix-x claude --permission-mode auto "Read /tmp/brief.md and do it."
  HC_SPACE=sp0123… hc-space --name fix-x-server --cwd ~/.forest/repo/fix-x "bun dev"
  ```

- **A short helper you wait on yourself** (a build, a test run, `gh run watch`): add
  `--no-parent` so it stays out of the window, and `--rm --no-notify` as hc's manual
  suggests. You lose parent notes, which `hc wait <t>` replaces.
- Only add a tab to the user's space when they ask for one.
- The space must be set at launch. `hc label` on a running terminal does not move it.
- `hc get <t>` shows a `where` label (`<space name> / <tab>`) once the window has
  placed it; check it after launching anything the user should see.
